Situation:
- Multiple supply depots are positioned across the area of operations, including Supply Depot Bravo and Depot Delta, holding a single critical supply class for immediate ground distribution. Supported units include FOB Kestrel (3rd Infantry Battalion), COP Archer (1st Armored Company), and other maneuver elements dispersed along contested main supply routes.
- Ground lines of communication are partially interdicted. Valley routes are exposed to observation and indirect fire; urban corridors face congestion and checkpoints. Weather and debris restrict convoy speeds and increase breakdown risk.
- Air resupply is constrained. All movements for this cycle will be ground-based with security escorts and route clearance.
- Engineer, EOD, and ISR support are limited. Each route that is opened for convoy movement requires a dedicated security and clearance package for the duration of use.

Mission:
- Deliver the critical supply class from available depots to all supported units within the current distribution cycle while minimizing total operational cost. Costs include transport, vehicle deployment, fixed route security risk for activated routes, and penalties for any authorized shortfalls.

Execution/Concept:
- Allocate outbound flow from each depot without exceeding available stocks. Prioritize sourcing from the most secure and cost-effective depots while maintaining overall responsiveness.
- Satisfy each unit’s demand; if required, accept controlled shortfalls at designated penalty rates. Shortfalls are a last resort when route risk, transport cost, or fleet constraints preclude full delivery.
- Match convoy loads to vehicle carrying capacity based on the supply’s volume. Vehicles cannot exceed capacity. Package and staging plans will align with load constraints and available loading windows.
- Activate only those depot-to-unit routes that are necessary. Activating a route (sending any vehicles on that route) incurs a fixed Risk Cost for security overhead—escort teams, route clearance, ISR overwatch, and QRF—independent of the number of vehicles subsequently dispatched on that route. Additional vehicles on an already-activated route incur only per-vehicle and transport costs.
- Enforce the fleet cap across all movements. Vehicles may only operate on routes activated for the cycle. Where feasible, consolidate deliveries to minimize the number of activated routes, balancing lower risk costs against potential increases in transport distance or shortfall penalties.
- The optimization objective is to minimize the sum of: transport costs by route, fixed route security risk costs for each activated route, per-vehicle deployment costs, and penalty costs for any unmet demand.

